<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- Chapter 6 answers.

     Same structure as ch2-answers.ptx: an <exercises> division, so every
     level is a dropdown. Exercise numbering restarts inside each
     <exercises> division, so the numbers here line up with
     ch6-exercises.ptx automatically. Keep the order of <exercise>
     elements (and of their tasks) identical to that file.
  -->

<exercises xml:id="ch6-answers" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Answers to Ch. 6 Exercises</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Answers to the exercises of this chapter. Click an
      exercise to open it, click a part to open that part, then click
      <term>Solution</term> to reveal the answer.
    </p>
    <p>
      These are the answers to the questions, not fully worked solutions. Enough is shown that you can
      tell whether your own work got to the correct conclusion.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="ans-hw-spne-threat">
    <title>Subgame Perfect Equilibria Practice</title>

    <task xml:id="ans-spne-threat-normal-form">
      <title>The normal form</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Write the game in normal form.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell><cell>Player 2: <m>xx</m></cell><cell>Player 2: <m>xy</m></cell><cell>Player 2: <m>yx</m></cell><cell>Player 2: <m>yy</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1: <m>U</m></cell><cell><m>(2, 3)</m></cell><cell><m>(2, 3)</m></cell><cell><m>(0, 1)</m></cell><cell><m>(0, 1)</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Player 1: <m>D</m></cell><cell><m>(1, 0)</m></cell><cell><m>(3, 2)</m></cell><cell><m>(1, 0)</m></cell><cell><m>(3, 2)</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="ans-spne-threat-nash">
      <title><m>(U, xx)</m> is a Nash equilibrium</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that <m>(U, xx)</m> is a Nash equilibrium.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Against <m>xx</m>, <m>U</m> is Player 1's best response (2 vs. 1). Against <m>U</m>, <m>xx</m> is Player 2's best response (3, the most she can get).
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="ans-spne-threat-subgames">
      <title>Checking each subgame</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Decide whether <m>(U, xx)</m> is a subgame perfect Nash equilibrium.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <em>Whole game:</em> a Nash equilibrium, by part (b).
        </p>
        <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell><cell>Player 2: <m>xx</m></cell><cell>Player 2: <m>xy</m></cell><cell>Player 2: <m>yx</m></cell><cell>Player 2: <m>yy</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1: <m>U</m></cell><cell><m>\boxed{(2, 3)}</m></cell><cell><m>(2, 3)</m></cell><cell><m>(0, 1)</m></cell><cell><m>(0, 1)</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Player 1: <m>D</m></cell><cell><m>(1, 0)</m></cell><cell><m>(3, 2)</m></cell><cell><m>(1, 0)</m></cell><cell><m>(3, 2)</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
        <p>
          <em>Subgame at <m>n_1</m>:</em> <m>xx</m> restricts to <m>x</m>. A Nash equilibrium, since <m>3 \gt 1</m>.
        </p>
        <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell>Subgame at <m>n_1</m></cell><cell>Player 2: <m>x</m></cell><cell>Player 2: <m>y</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Payoffs</cell><cell><m>\boxed{(2, 3)}</m></cell><cell><m>(0, 1)</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
        <p>
          <em>Subgame at <m>n_2</m>:</em> <m>xx</m> restricts to <m>x</m>. Not a Nash equilibrium, since <m>y</m> gives Player 2 a payoff of 2, more than 0.
        </p>
        <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell>Subgame at <m>n_2</m></cell><cell>Player 2: <m>x</m></cell><cell>Player 2: <m>y</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Payoffs</cell><cell><m>\boxed{(1, 0)}</m></cell><cell><m>(3, 2)</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
        <p>
          So <m>(U, xx)</m> is not a subgame perfect Nash equilibrium.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="ans-spne-threat-words">
      <title>Backward induction and the threat</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the subgame perfect Nash equilibrium, compare it with <m>(U, xx)</m>, and explain the threat.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The subgame perfect Nash equilibrium is <m>(D, xy)</m>, with outcome <m>(3, 2)</m>. The outcome of <m>(U, xx)</m> is <m>(2, 3)</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Playing <m>x</m> at <m>n_2</m> is a threat because it hurts Player 1 (1 instead of 3) and also hurts Player 2 (0 instead of 2). Player 2 benefits: it gets Player 1 to choose <m>U</m>, so Player 2 gets 3 instead of 2. It only works if Player 1 believes it: if Player 1 chooses <m>D</m>, Player 2's best move at <m>n_2</m> is <m>y</m>, which gives Player 1 a payoff of 3.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>
  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="ans-hw-trigger-and-tit-for-tat">
    <title>Trigger and Tit for Tat</title>

    <task xml:id="ans-trigger-tft-words">
      <title>The trigger strategy in words</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Explain in words what it means for Player 1 to use <m>\sigma</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Player 1 starts out cooperating, and keeps cooperating as long as Player 2 cooperates. As soon as Player 2 defects once, Player 1 defects in every round from then on, no matter what Player 2 does after that.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="ans-trigger-tft-sigma-tau">
      <title><m>\pi_1(\sigma, \tau)</m> and <m>\pi_2(\sigma, \tau)</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>\pi_1(\sigma, \tau)</m> and <m>\pi_2(\sigma, \tau)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Both players play <m>c</m> in every round, so <m>\pi_1(\sigma, \tau) = \pi_2(\sigma, \tau) = \frac{5}{1 - \delta}</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="ans-trigger-tft-tau-nu">
      <title><m>\pi_1(\tau, \nu)</m> and <m>\pi_2(\tau, \nu)</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>\pi_1(\tau, \nu)</m> and <m>\pi_2(\tau, \nu)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell><cell>round 0</cell><cell>round 1</cell><cell>round 2</cell><cell>round 3</cell><cell>round 4</cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1's move (<m>\tau</m>)</cell><cell><m>c</m></cell><cell><m>d</m></cell><cell><m>c</m></cell><cell><m>d</m></cell><cell><m>c</m></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 2's move (<m>\nu</m>)</cell><cell><m>d</m></cell><cell><m>c</m></cell><cell><m>d</m></cell><cell><m>c</m></cell><cell><m>d</m></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1's payoff</cell><cell><m>-8</m></cell><cell><m>8</m></cell><cell><m>-8</m></cell><cell><m>8</m></cell><cell><m>-8</m></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Player 2's payoff</cell><cell><m>8</m></cell><cell><m>-8</m></cell><cell><m>8</m></cell><cell><m>-8</m></cell><cell><m>8</m></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
        <p>
          <m>\pi_1(\tau, \nu) = \frac{-8}{1 + \delta}</m> and <m>\pi_2(\tau, \nu) = \frac{8}{1 + \delta}</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="ans-trigger-tft-threshold">
      <title>When <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m> is a Nash equilibrium</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>\delta_0</m> such that <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m> is a Nash equilibrium whenever <m>\delta \geq \delta_0</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>\delta_0 = \frac{3}{8}</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>
  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="ans-hw-tit-for-tat-equilibrium">
    <title>Tit for Tat Against Other Strategies</title>

    <task xml:id="ans-tft-eq-compare">
      <title><m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m> compared with <m>(\tau, \tau)</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Compare the payoffs for <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m> and <m>(\tau, \tau)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          They are the same: both players play <m>c</m> in every round, so each gets <m>\frac{5}{1 - \delta}</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="ans-tft-eq-nu">
      <title>Switching from <m>\tau</m> to <m>\nu</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>\delta_0</m> such that <m>\pi_1(\nu, \tau) \leq \pi_1(\tau, \tau)</m> whenever <m>\delta \geq \delta_0</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>\pi_1(\nu, \tau) = \frac{8}{1 + \delta}</m>, and <m>\delta_0 = \frac{3}{13}</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="ans-tft-eq-beta">
      <title>Switching from <m>\tau</m> to <m>\beta</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>\delta_0</m> such that <m>\pi_1(\beta, \tau) \leq \pi_1(\tau, \tau)</m> whenever <m>\delta \geq \delta_0</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell><cell>round 0</cell><cell>round 1</cell><cell>round 2</cell><cell>round 3</cell><cell>round 4</cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1's move (<m>\beta</m>)</cell><cell><m>d</m></cell><cell><m>d</m></cell><cell><m>d</m></cell><cell><m>d</m></cell><cell><m>d</m></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 2's move (<m>\tau</m>)</cell><cell><m>c</m></cell><cell><m>d</m></cell><cell><m>d</m></cell><cell><m>d</m></cell><cell><m>d</m></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Player 1's payoff</cell><cell><m>8</m></cell><cell><m>0</m></cell><cell><m>0</m></cell><cell><m>0</m></cell><cell><m>0</m></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
        <p>
          <m>\pi_1(\beta, \tau) = 8</m>, and <m>\delta_0 = \frac{3}{8}</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>
  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="ans-hw-tit-for-tat-nash-not-spne">
    <title>Tit for Tat Is a Nash Equilibrium, but Not Subgame Perfect</title>

    <task xml:id="ans-tft-spne-table">
      <title>Payoffs during an excursion</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Fill in Player 1's payoff (with discount factor) in each round of an excursion.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell><cell>round 0</cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell><cell>round <m>k-1</m></cell><cell>round <m>k</m></cell><cell>round <m>k+1</m></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell><cell>round <m>k+m-1</m></cell><cell>round <m>k+m</m></cell><cell>round <m>k+m+1</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1's move</cell><cell colspan="3"><m>c</m></cell><cell><m>d</m></cell><cell><m>d</m></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell><cell><m>d</m></cell><cell><m>c</m></cell><cell><m>c</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 2's move (<m>\tau</m>)</cell><cell colspan="3"><m>c</m></cell><cell><m>c</m></cell><cell><m>d</m></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell><cell><m>d</m></cell><cell><m>d</m></cell><cell><m>c</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1's payoff (with discount factor)</cell><cell colspan="3"><m>5, 5\delta, \ldots, 5\delta^{k-1}</m></cell><cell><m>8\delta^k</m></cell><cell><m>0</m></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell><cell><m>0</m></cell><cell><m>-8\delta^{k+m}</m></cell><cell><m>5\delta^{k+m+1}</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Player 1's payoff (with discount factor) if they had kept using <m>\tau</m></cell><cell colspan="3"><m>5, 5\delta, \ldots, 5\delta^{k-1}</m></cell><cell><m>5\delta^k</m></cell><cell><m>5\delta^{k+1}</m></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell><cell><m>5\delta^{k+m-1}</m></cell><cell><m>5\delta^{k+m}</m></cell><cell><m>5\delta^{k+m+1}</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="ans-tft-spne-inequality">
      <title>The excursion doesn't help</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that the excursion does not improve Player 1's payoff in rounds <m>k</m> through <m>k + m</m> when <m>\delta \geq \frac{3}{8}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Canceling <m>\delta^k</m>, we need <m>8 - 8\delta^m \leq 5(1 + \delta + \cdots + \delta^m)</m>. When <m>\delta \geq \frac{3}{8}</m>,
        </p>
        <md>
          <mrow>5(1 + \delta + \cdots + \delta^m) \amp = \frac{5}{1 - \delta}\left(1 - \delta^{m+1}\right) \amp \amp \text{(finite geometric series)}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp \geq 8\left(1 - \delta^{m+1}\right) \amp \amp \left(\tfrac{5}{1 - \delta} \geq 8 \text{ since } \delta \geq \tfrac{3}{8}\right)</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp \geq 8\left(1 - \delta^m\right) \amp \amp \left(\delta^{m+1} \leq \delta^m\right)</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp = 8 - 8\delta^m.</mrow>
        </md>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="ans-tft-spne-reset">
      <title>Every way of switching away from <m>\tau</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Explain why several excursions are worse than <m>\tau</m>, and which earlier part handles defecting forever.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <ol marker="i.">
          <li>
            <p>
              After each excursion the game starts over, so each excursion can be compared with <m>\tau</m> on its own rounds. By part (b), each one gives Player 1 less than cooperating would, and every other round is the same as with <m>\tau</m>. So several excursions give Player 1 less than <m>\tau</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <xref ref="hw-tit-for-tat-equilibrium-beta"/>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="ans-tft-spne-subgame">
      <title><m>(\tau, \tau)</m> is not subgame perfect</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          In the subgame after <m>(c, d)</m> in round 0, compare <m>\tau</m> and <m>\zeta</m> for Player 1.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <ol marker="i.">
          <li>
        <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell><cell><line>round 1</line><line>(after <m>(c, d)</m>)</line></cell><cell>round 2</cell><cell>round 3</cell><cell>round 4</cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1's move (<m>\tau</m>)</cell><cell><m>d</m></cell><cell><m>c</m></cell><cell><m>d</m></cell><cell><m>c</m></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 2's move (<m>\tau</m>)</cell><cell><m>c</m></cell><cell><m>d</m></cell><cell><m>c</m></cell><cell><m>d</m></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Player 1's payoff (with discount factor)</cell><cell><m>8\delta</m></cell><cell><m>-8\delta^2</m></cell><cell><m>8\delta^3</m></cell><cell><m>-8\delta^4</m></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
            <p>
              <m>\frac{8\delta}{1 + \delta}</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
        <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell><cell><line>round 1</line><line>(after <m>(c, d)</m>)</line></cell><cell>round 2</cell><cell>round 3</cell><cell>round 4</cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1's move (<m>\zeta</m>)</cell><cell><m>c</m></cell><cell><m>c</m></cell><cell><m>c</m></cell><cell><m>c</m></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 2's move (<m>\tau</m>)</cell><cell><m>c</m></cell><cell><m>c</m></cell><cell><m>c</m></cell><cell><m>c</m></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Player 1's payoff (with discount factor)</cell><cell><m>5\delta</m></cell><cell><m>5\delta^2</m></cell><cell><m>5\delta^3</m></cell><cell><m>5\delta^4</m></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
            <p>
              <m>\frac{5\delta}{1 - \delta}</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\delta \gt \frac{3}{13}</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              No, the restriction is not a Nash equilibrium. No, <m>(\tau, \tau)</m> is not subgame perfect.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>
  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="ans-hw-common-fund">
    <title>Contributing to a Common Fund</title>

    <task xml:id="ans-common-fund-payoff">
      <title>The payoff function</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the payoff function <m>\pi_i(x_i, x_{-i})</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <me>\pi_i(x_i, x_{-i}) = 1 - x_i + \frac{3}{4}(x_1 + x_2 + x_3 + x_4).</me>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="ans-common-fund-dominated">
      <title>Contributing nothing is strictly dominant</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that <m>x_i \gt 0</m> is strictly dominated by <m>x_i = 0</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>\pi_i = 1 - \frac{1}{4}x_i + \frac{3}{4}(\text{sum of the others' contributions})</m>, which decreases as <m>x_i</m> increases, whatever the others contribute. So <m>x_i = 0</m> gives the largest payoff.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="ans-common-fund-three-payoffs">
      <title>Three payoffs</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find Player <m>i</m>'s payoff in each of the three situations.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <ol marker="i.">
          <li>
            <p>
              Everyone contributes <m>y</m>: <m>1 - y + \frac{3}{4}(4y) = 1 + 2y</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Player <m>i</m> contributes 0 and the others contribute <m>y</m>: <m>1 + \frac{3}{4}(3y) = 1 + \frac{9}{4}y</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Everyone contributes 0: <m>1</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="ans-common-fund-trigger">
      <title>When <m>(\sigma_y, \sigma_y, \sigma_y, \sigma_y)</m> is a Nash equilibrium</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>\delta_0</m> such that <m>(\sigma_y, \sigma_y, \sigma_y, \sigma_y)</m> is a Nash equilibrium whenever <m>\delta \geq \delta_0</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Solve
        </p>
        <me>\frac{1 + 2y}{1 - \delta} \geq 1 + \frac{9}{4}y + \frac{\delta}{1 - \delta}</me>
        <p>
          to get <m>\delta_0 = \frac{1}{9}</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="ans-common-fund-subgame">
      <title>Subgame perfection</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Decide whether <m>(\sigma_y, \sigma_y, \sigma_y, \sigma_y)</m> is a subgame perfect Nash equilibrium when <m>\delta \geq \delta_0</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <ol marker="i.">
          <li>
            <p>
              Yes, when <m>\delta \geq \frac{1}{9}</m>. Everyone contributes <m>y</m>, so the subgame looks like the whole game with every payoff multiplied by <m>\delta^k</m>, and part (d) applies.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Yes. Everyone contributes 0 forever, and by part (b) contributing 0 is a best response in every round.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Yes, when <m>\delta \geq \frac{1}{9}</m>, since every subgame falls into one of the two cases.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</exercises>
